WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.146 --> 00:00:05.463
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.463 --> 00:00:10.757
I want to take you back to the year 2010,
it's my senior year of high school and

00:00:10.757 --> 00:00:15.020
I'm in the final round of
a speech team tournament.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:18.387
Yes, I was the nerd in high school who
did speech he considered it a sport.

00:00:18.387 --> 00:00:21.552
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; There are five of the finalists in

00:00:21.552 --> 00:00:25.823
the room, 30 people there to watch us and
three judges sitting in the front row with

00:00:25.823 --> 00:00:29.020
their pens and
evaluation forms ready to go.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:32.739
And I'm there to give an eight
minute speech on racial diversity.

00:00:32.739 --> 00:00:35.951
So I deliver my speech,
listened to my competitors do the same,

00:00:35.951 --> 00:00:38.754
gave them a cute little bit
of applause and a fake smile.

00:00:38.754 --> 00:00:41.133
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:45.930
then we all headed to the auditorium
to hear the final results, and

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:49.713
in first place, they announced,
Shawan Jackson.

00:00:49.713 --> 00:00:52.702
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Of course I share that story because I

00:00:52.702 --> 00:00:54.226
want you to know how fabulous I am.

00:00:54.226 --> 00:00:54.925
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] But

00:00:54.925 --> 00:00:59.910
really I share that story because
it was a moment of pride back then.

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:04.220
But when I look back on that moment today,

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:08.083
I'm not met with pride,
I actually feel regret.

00:01:08.083 --> 00:01:11.932
What I didn't mention at the beginning
of my talk is that, that speech

00:01:11.932 --> 00:01:16.250
wasn't advocating for racial diversity,
I essentially argued against it.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.793
To be fair,
I didn't say that diversity itself is bad,

00:01:20.793 --> 00:01:25.182
I just didn't understand the merits
of racial diversity in particular.

00:01:25.182 --> 00:01:30.310
In large part because I didn't understand
the full extent of racial inequity.

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:34.370
Reflecting on this moment, I realized that
while I had strong communication skills

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:37.390
back then,
I lacked critical consciousness.

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:42.316
A term coined by Paulo Freire, a Brazilian
philosopher that speaks to one's

00:01:42.316 --> 00:01:46.880
ability to critically examine the world
and do something to change it.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.305
Realizing this inspired me to help other
youth, especially underserved youth,

00:01:51.305 --> 00:01:56.530
build not just their communication skills,
but also their critical consciousness.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:01.310
With the hope that they'll be able to
speak persuasively about social justice.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:02.795
But in doing this work,

00:02:02.795 --> 00:02:08.447
I've realized that critical consciousness
isn't just about challenging what you say,

00:02:08.447 --> 00:02:12.930
it's also about challenging how you
present yourself and your ideas.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:18.141
Now when I first started teaching public
speaking, I did not realize this,

00:02:18.141 --> 00:02:20.901
I used to teach my students
the control framework.

00:02:20.901 --> 00:02:24.786
It went a little something like this,
control your body stand up straight, relax

00:02:24.786 --> 00:02:28.920
your shoulders, make sure they're in line
with your feet gesture, but not too much.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:33.402
Control your voice, speak up,
pace yourself pause so that people really

00:02:33.402 --> 00:02:35.560
listen, okay, not that long-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:38.807
&gt;&gt; And enunciate every single word as you

00:02:38.807 --> 00:02:39.521
speak.

00:02:39.521 --> 00:02:42.734
Now research shows that if
you do those behaviors,

00:02:42.734 --> 00:02:45.284
you actually do sound more persuasive.

00:02:45.284 --> 00:02:48.433
But the more I've been working
with youth of color, and

00:02:48.433 --> 00:02:52.051
the more I've been reflecting
on my own public speaking style,

00:02:52.051 --> 00:02:55.870
I realized that there's a point
when control becomes conformity,

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:59.090
when the right way to speak
becomes the white way to do it.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:04.551
Now let me be clear I don't dislike the
way that white people speak, I love y'all.

00:03:04.551 --> 00:03:07.074
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:03:07.074 --> 00:03:10.985
it saddens me that we have this
unstated expectation that,

00:03:10.985 --> 00:03:15.922
to be heard you have to make yourself
palatable to white majority culture.

00:03:15.922 --> 00:03:19.514
Maybe that means cutting out the black
vernacular you learned at home because

00:03:19.514 --> 00:03:21.640
it's not professional.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:26.361
Or maybe that means limiting your
expressions because you were told you need

00:03:26.361 --> 00:03:27.401
to tone it down.

00:03:27.401 --> 00:03:30.930
Or maybe it's cutting your Afro,
like I did in the eighth grade,

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:33.510
because you learn that
it's not presentable.

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:39.181
These behaviors reflect the concept
called respectability politics,

00:03:39.181 --> 00:03:43.724
when marginalized people present
themselves in ways that are pleasing or

00:03:43.724 --> 00:03:46.448
respectable to those with more privilege.

00:03:46.448 --> 00:03:51.682
On the one hand, this makes
complete sense, our world favors

00:03:51.682 --> 00:03:56.816
those who just fit in, and
as important as authenticity is,

00:03:56.816 --> 00:04:03.280
it comes with a risk of being judged,
of being ignored, of being silenced.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:07.545
And this is a risk that many people
care about, not just people of color.

00:04:07.545 --> 00:04:12.781
According to a 2014 inclusion report
from Deloitte, 83% of lesbian,

00:04:12.781 --> 00:04:18.115
gay and bisexual people in the US,
said they're not fully themselves at work.

00:04:18.115 --> 00:04:23.250
66% of woman said the same, and
so did 44% of straight White men.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:28.001
Many of us feel this pressure to conform,

00:04:28.001 --> 00:04:33.114
to avoid the risk of exclusion,
but conformity comes with a cost.

00:04:33.114 --> 00:04:35.360
Every time we change how we present,

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:40.214
from the words we use to our tone to our
presentation, we validate the status quo

00:04:40.214 --> 00:04:44.217
that is not equally appreciate
multiple forms of presentation.

00:04:44.217 --> 00:04:48.163
We send a message to younger
generations that you can be heard, so

00:04:48.163 --> 00:04:52.200
long as you sound look and
dress more or less, like everyone else.

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:57.152
The easy answer would be what don't
conform when you communicate,

00:04:57.152 --> 00:04:59.267
but it's not that straightforward.

00:04:59.267 --> 00:05:02.843
Because at times you do need to
accommodate your audience, so

00:05:02.843 --> 00:05:05.337
that you can make your perspective heard.

00:05:05.337 --> 00:05:10.236
I don't know the answer to this question
of how to deal with that, on the one hand,

00:05:10.236 --> 00:05:13.940
I do want to be authentic,
on the other hand, I want to be heard.

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:16.372
Even as I'm standing here
in front of you right now,

00:05:16.372 --> 00:05:20.078
I'm wondering if the way I'm dressed and
the way I'm presenting is really me or

00:05:20.078 --> 00:05:21.709
just what I think you want me to be.

00:05:21.709 --> 00:05:23.902
And if I can't answer that question for
myself,

00:05:23.902 --> 00:05:25.680
who am I to talk to my students about it?

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:30.828
One of my mentors said, you don't need
to have the perfect answer, Shawan.

00:05:30.828 --> 00:05:33.136
Just open up the conversation
with your students, so

00:05:33.136 --> 00:05:35.050
they can critically engage with the topic.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:38.692
So now in one of my classes
on public speaking,

00:05:38.692 --> 00:05:42.808
I read a series of statements and
ask students go to one side if you agree,

00:05:42.808 --> 00:05:47.418
another side of you disagree and somewhere
in the middle if you're conflicted.

00:05:47.418 --> 00:05:51.179
Agree or disagree, if I were giving
a speech in front of politicians,

00:05:51.179 --> 00:05:54.910
I would change my hair so
that I look more professional.

00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:59.615
Students spread out across the entire
room and began to debate their opinions.

00:05:59.615 --> 00:06:01.051
One of my Latino student said,

00:06:01.051 --> 00:06:05.200
I'm not going to straighten my curly
because that's a part of who I am.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:08.304
Another student respectfully disagrees and
says,

00:06:08.304 --> 00:06:11.476
it's not that big of a deal to me,
so I'm going to do it,

00:06:11.476 --> 00:06:16.150
we don't come to a perfect answer in
that activity, but that's not the goal.

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:19.720
The goal is simply to question
whether persuasion can be problematic.

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:25.630
In the same activity I read another
statement, agree or disagree,

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:31.070
to make people care about a social issue,
I will share a sad story about myself.

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:35.021
This leads to a critical
conversation about storytelling,

00:06:35.021 --> 00:06:40.150
too often we share those sad stories so
that people feel bad for us.

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:42.567
And while those stories can be moving,

00:06:42.567 --> 00:06:47.119
they can often fall into this trap of
poverty porn, you know what that is.

00:06:47.119 --> 00:06:51.217
Those TV ads about those poor little
kids in Africa who need your donation,

00:06:51.217 --> 00:06:55.510
that story about a boy named Tony, who
grew up in Chicago in a rundown apartment

00:06:55.510 --> 00:06:58.307
in a single parent home who
will not make it in life,

00:06:58.307 --> 00:07:00.350
unless you donate to his nonprofit.

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:02.413
That's poverty porn.

00:07:02.413 --> 00:07:07.490
Storytelling is a powerful tool of
persuasion, but the problem with poverty

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:12.583
porn is that, it reduces your humanities
to the struggles you've endured.

00:07:12.583 --> 00:07:17.213
Without critically examining the
narratives we're putting forward, we risk

00:07:17.213 --> 00:07:22.130
diluting our self-worth, presenting an
incomplete picture of who we really are.

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:26.300
And that's a problem, when I was
teaching public speaking last summer,

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:29.960
I had a student who I won't soon forget,
Jose.

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:33.278
He's a junior now,
Dominican American incredibly smart,

00:07:33.278 --> 00:07:35.496
and he ditched about half of my classes.

00:07:35.496 --> 00:07:38.958
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So one day, I'm in the hallway with

00:07:38.958 --> 00:07:43.029
him and I take them to the side,
just to say what's going on.

00:07:43.029 --> 00:07:47.492
He told me he didn't like public speaking,
I figured just about as much, but

00:07:47.492 --> 00:07:49.770
his reason why is what really got to me.

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:55.139
It doesn't matter how
much we speak up he said,

00:07:55.139 --> 00:08:00.461
at the end of the day, rich,
white people will always be in power.

00:08:00.461 --> 00:08:03.843
Deep down I fear that Jose is right,
not because rich,

00:08:03.843 --> 00:08:08.407
white people will literally always be
in power, but because if we're not

00:08:08.407 --> 00:08:12.824
mindful of how we're presenting,
how we're advocating for change,

00:08:12.824 --> 00:08:17.850
we risk changing the people in power, but
leaving the culture largely the same.

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:23.668
So if I were talking to younger Shawan
today, I would tell them two things one,

00:08:23.668 --> 00:08:28.227
you're going to have that baby face for
the next 10 years, at least, I'm sorry.

00:08:28.227 --> 00:08:31.314
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It is what it is.

00:08:31.314 --> 00:08:35.268
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And two and more importantly,

00:08:35.268 --> 00:08:38.816
I would say that it's not
enough to change your topic for

00:08:38.816 --> 00:08:44.268
that speech team competition, to argue for
racial diversity instead of against it.

00:08:44.268 --> 00:08:49.045
You also need to critically question how
you're presenting yourself in that room,

00:08:49.045 --> 00:08:52.470
are you making yourself
conforming too much?

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:55.117
How far are you willing to go?

00:08:55.117 --> 00:08:59.492
And I want Shawan to think about those
questions as a teenager because ten

00:08:59.492 --> 00:09:03.955
years later I want him to feel free and
comfortable to speak his own truth.

00:09:05.195 --> 00:09:09.726
The entire truth, not just because
it will make me feel better, but

00:09:09.726 --> 00:09:13.385
because when you speak your truth,
your whole truth,

00:09:13.385 --> 00:09:17.696
you empower everyone else around
you to do the same, thank you.

00:09:17.696 --> 00:09:18.814
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:18.814 --> 00:09:23.640
[MUSIC]

